  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 107 AM MDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nSunday: Heading into Sunday, another relatively quiet weather day is \nin store for south central and southeastern Colorado. Again, overall \nridging will be in place. With the drier air and subsidence with \nthis pattern, dry conditions are expected for the area. With that \nsaid, weak embedded shortwaves along the eastern periphery of an \napproaching trough will start to push over the area Sunday night. \nWith the uptick in forcing from these features, isolated to \nscattered showers are anticipated to blossom along the higher \nterrain, but particularly the Continental Divide. Along with that, \nclouds will steadily increase throughout the day in response to the \napproaching troughing, along with winds also starting to increase, \nespecially across the plains. Temperatures throughout Sunday will \nmaintain the above seasonal temperature trend, especially across the \nplains.\n\nMonday - Friday: For next week, active weather makes a return to \nsouth central and southeastern Colorado, especially during midweek. \nA fairly dynamic pattern is expected to play out next week, as the \naforementioned approaching trough starts pushing over and the \nremnants of ongoing hurricane Polo interact. At this point, model \nguidance is in solid agreement about arcing hurricane Polo to the NE \nand pulling it into the broader troughing flow. With that said \nthough, there is still some uncertainty in the timing of phasing and \nhow well the systems phase at all. Regardless though, with the surge \nin both forcing and moisture from this pattern/interaction, \nprecipitation chances are expected to increase substantially \nareawide, but especially across the plains. If these systems phase \nin a constructive way, there is even potential for this to be a high \nimpact pattern in regards to flooding. With that all said though, as \nmentioned, uncertainty does still remain, and the best thing to do \nfor now is to continue to monitor the forecast for the latest \nupdates. Perhaps if we start yelling out \"Marco\" we'll hear the \n\"Polo\" call outs back to us, and we can get a better idea of where \nthe storm will ultimately go! Beyond all of that, winds and cloud \ncover are expected to increase given the unsettled pattern, though \nwith both lessening by the end of next week as the pattern pushes \neast. Looking at temperatures, Monday will be the warmest day ahead \nof the busier pattern, with a substantial cool down thereafter \ninfluenced by frontal passage and the overall unsettled pattern.",
